## Current Synthesis

### Idea

Replace Tool Shed's all-inclusive inline context delivery for Codex App Server planning with a
bounded, reference-oriented retrieval design. Tool Shed should send a small deterministic planning
capsule first, expose only an allowlisted read-only context snapshot, and let the planner retrieve
specific files or ranges as needed under measurable per-result and cumulative budgets.

The observed failure, `focused context exceeded its 100 KB inline limit before any model request`,
is a Tool Shed admission failure rather than evidence of a Codex model context-window failure. The
current repository config sets `max_inline_bytes` to 100,000, and Tool Shed checks that value before
calling the App Server. Automatic CAMP preparation is intentionally tighter: a 48,000-byte planning
snapshot and at most 64,000 bytes of selected context files.

Codex App Server already supports read-only filesystem boundaries, focused working directories,
threads, and explicit compaction. It does not currently document a generic local-document input
item comparable to `localImage`, so "pass by reference" likely means allowing controlled reads from
an isolated snapshot rather than merely placing an arbitrary host path in the turn payload.

### Why It Matters

Moderately complicated Tool Shed planning can exceed 100 KB even though the selected model can
accept substantially more context. Rejecting such work before the model sees it prevents App Server
from reducing GUI-session token use on exactly the larger jobs where a separate bounded worker is
most valuable.

Simply raising the byte limit would preserve the underlying inefficiency: Tool Shed would continue
serializing every selected file into one prompt, spend tokens on material the planner may not need,
and fail again at a larger threshold. The desired gain comes from progressive relevance-based
retrieval, not from moving one large context blob from the GUI to App Server.

This also needs a safety-preserving solution. Current automatic preparation deliberately gives the
planner no tools and treats its supplied snapshot as complete. Introducing retrieval changes that
trust boundary and must not permit repository mutation, network access, parent-workspace discovery,
or unbounded tool output.

### Desired Outcome

A Tool Shed planning operation can handle a repository whose potentially relevant material exceeds
the inline cap without weakening planning correctness or CAMP safety. Before the first model
request, Tool Shed constructs a compact manifest containing intent, exact snapshot identity, Git
state, candidate paths, byte sizes, content digests, budgets, and authority boundaries. The planner
then reads only necessary allowlisted content through a bounded read-only mechanism.

The resulting plan or execution capsule remains source-bound, deterministic, and independently
validated. Every retrieval records the path or content identity, requested range, returned bytes,
and digest without retaining unnecessary raw output in durable telemetry. Tool Shed measures total
input tokens, retrieved bytes, turns, latency, task success, and fallback rate against the current
inline implementation.

When the bounded planner cannot establish adequate context, it returns a controlled
`needs_more_context` or equivalent result. Tool Shed either expands the allowed snapshot within a
defined budget, starts a compact continuation, or immediately falls back to the normal GUI route.
It never converts missing context into a guessed plan or lifecycle advance.

## Constraints And Non-Goals

- Preserve the existing project identity, Git journal, path allowlist, source-state token,
  exactly-once verification, no-retry-after-mutation, and fail-closed lifecycle rules.
- Planning retrieval is read-only, workspace-bounded, network-disabled, and unavailable to paths
  outside the isolated snapshot or explicit readable roots.
- Bound each retrieval and the cumulative bytes/tokens/turns. Do not make one giant read equivalent
  to the current inline prompt.
- A file path alone is not proof the model received or read its contents. Record actual retrieval
  evidence and source digests.
- References and compaction are context-management mechanisms, not proof of lower token use; the
  qualification must measure savings on representative planning tasks.
- Keep model context size and App Server protocol capabilities version-observed rather than frozen
  as permanent Tool Shed assumptions.
- Do not replace deterministic repository inventory, relevance scoring, or capsule validation with
  model judgment.
- Do not enable unrestricted shell access, network access, workspace mutation, production changes,
  or a general-purpose retrieval service as part of this Idea.
- Do not treat increasing `max_inline_bytes` as the primary solution.

## Possibilities And Tradeoffs

| Possibility | Benefits | Costs / Risks | Evidence / Status |
| --- | --- | --- | --- |
| Raise the inline byte limit | Minimal implementation change; immediately admits larger prompts | Higher token cost, repeated irrelevant context, merely moves the failure threshold | Rejected as the primary design; may remain an emergency tuning control |
| Copy allowlisted files into a focused read-only snapshot and let Codex read them | Reuses existing focused-snapshot and App Server sandbox concepts; natural repository workflow | Read operations and outputs must be tightly bounded and auditable | Preferred foundation |
| Add a dedicated `read_context(path, start, limit)` capability | Precise path/range limits, compact telemetry, no general shell | New protocol/adapter surface and cross-platform qualification | Preferred safety shape unless existing App Server file tools prove equally enforceable |
| Precompute summaries for every candidate file | Very small prompts and predictable cost | Summaries may omit decisive details or become stale; generation itself costs work | Useful only as a digest-bound optional index, never sole authority |
| Split one large inline prompt across turns | Avoids one-request byte admission limit | Still consumes the full context and complicates continuity | Insufficient by itself |
| Fall back to GUI for oversized or unresolved planning | Preserves correctness while retrieval matures | Does not achieve low-token App Server execution for those cases | Required fail-safe |

## Open Questions

- Should the first implementation use Codex's existing read-only filesystem tools against copied
  snapshots, or expose a dedicated range-reading tool with a smaller capability surface?
- What information must always be inline so the planner can select context without first knowing
  repository contents: campaign text, project instructions, file inventory, symbol index, Git
  status, recent diffs, or bounded excerpts?
- What per-read, per-turn, and complete-operation limits preserve useful planning while ensuring a
  retrieval loop cannot consume more tokens than the GUI route it is intended to replace?
- When should Tool Shed expand a snapshot, compact and continue the same thread, start a new bounded
  planning thread, or fall back immediately to GUI?
- How will Tool Shed prove that a successful plan did not silently omit a required dependency or
  instruction file? Candidate approaches include dependency fixtures, deliberate decoy files, and
  comparison with a full-context oracle on a bounded qualification corpus.
- Can one reference/retrieval contract behave identically on Linux and Windows despite path,
  encoding, newline, and sandbox differences?
- Should repeated unchanged content use prompt caching, local digest-addressed summaries, or both,
  and what invalidation evidence is required?

## Decisions

- Treat the 100 KB failure as Tool Shed's current inline admission limit, not as a demonstrated
  Codex App Server transport or model limit.
- Optimize for bounded progressive retrieval rather than a larger monolithic inline prompt.
- Start every planning run from a deterministic, digest-bound manifest and an isolated allowlisted
  read-only snapshot.
- Prefer a narrow context-read interface over unrestricted shell access when it can provide the
  required repository understanding.
- Preserve explicit GUI fallback whenever bounded retrieval cannot prove sufficient context.
- Evaluate the design on correctness, completeness, token use, latency, and fallback rate; fewer
  tokens alone do not qualify a worse plan.
- Keep planning/retrieval separate from write-capable CAMP execution and retain all existing CAMP
  mutation and verification controls.

## Don't Forget

- The current configuration already contains a `reference` delivery mode for generic focused
  contexts, but the active configuration selects `inline_relevant_files`; automatic preparation
  separately forces inline deterministic context and prohibits tools.
- `thread/inject_items` still places content in model-visible history; it is not an external
  zero-token reference store.
- Thread compaction helps long-running conversations but does not fix a pre-request Tool Shed byte
  guard on the first planning payload.
- Tool output can become the new context bottleneck unless reads return bounded ranges and compact
  metadata.
- Relevant project instructions and authority boundaries must be available before optional source
  retrieval so a small prompt does not become an under-specified prompt.
- Compare against real planning failures above 100 KB, not only synthetic byte-count tests.
- No currently open GitHub issue was found that directly owns this context-delivery redesign.

